00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:45.370
So Istio is a service mesh.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:47.410
And you're probably
hearing this term

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.770
if you look at the Google
Trends on service mesh,

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:55.610
it is an area of big interest
and increasing interest.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:06:00.400
And I want to describe a service
mesh in a few number of words.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.380
The important things
about a service mesh

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:06.280
is that it automates
away a lot of the things

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.690
that we typically build on
top of our network stacks.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:13.120
Automates away
things like seeing

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:16.930
what's going on in terms of
traffic to your services,

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.560
providing authentication and
authorization on top of those.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.460
So it does that, but
very importantly,

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:23.980
to be considered a
service mesh, you

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.950
have to do that in
a transparent way.

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:29.770
A service mesh isn't something
you code to, necessarily.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:32.530
In fact, most developers should
never even know it's there.

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:34.249
It has to be something
that can layer

00:06:34.249 --> 00:06:36.040
in on top of whatever
you're already doing.

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:37.840
Whatever language
you're already using,

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.120
whatever platform you
like to deploy on.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.070
The idea of a service
mesh is to automate

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:46.790
a lot of the functionality out
of the network, up-level what

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:48.970
the network gives
you in a completely

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:52.280
transparent and
language-independent way.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:54.970
And Istio is not
just a service mesh

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:56.865
but it's an open
services platform.

00:06:56.865 --> 00:06:58.240
There are a few
different service

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.489
meshes that have come out
in the last couple of years.

00:07:00.489 --> 00:07:04.270
And where Istio differentiates
is by defining a set of APIs

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:08.050
that make it easy to integrate
back-end systems as well.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:11.200
So arbitrary, you log
wherever you want to,

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:12.970
you monitor wherever
you want to,

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:16.820
you use whatever arbitrary
policy engines you want to.

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:20.670
And again, all your services
are taking advantage of that

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:24.160
without coding themselves,
without integrating

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.910
into the service
mesh explicitly.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:32.240
We talk about it a lot in
the context of Kubernetes,

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.320
but Istio is not dependent
on-- it's not dependent

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.570
that you only deploy
workloads in Kubernetes.

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:40.390
We do have people who are
deploying workloads across VMs

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:40.990
as well.

00:07:43.269 --> 00:07:44.060
So what does it do?

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:46.268
What is the functionality
that it offers you once you

00:07:46.268 --> 00:07:48.140
layer in this
transparent service mesh?

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:50.240
The first thing
is observability.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.310
It's kind of unbelievable
how many people out

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:54.260
there are deploying
apps into Kubernetes

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:58.070
and have no idea what's going
on in those applications.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:00.680
It gives you good
visibility into what's

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.660
happening in a
distributed application.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.140
The second thing, it
says operational agility.

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:07.760
And by operational
agility what I mean

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:10.880
is being able to control
where a request goes.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.010
And be able to have
a safe and sane way

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:16.230
to roll out new versions of
services as you're doing that.

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.071
And be able to
control, hey, maybe we

00:08:18.071 --> 00:08:19.820
need to send traffic
to a different region

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:23.750
right now because this one
is very busy and overloaded.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:26.000
And then the third major
area of functionality

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.950
is security, is allowing you to
secure traffic between services

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:33.350
and ensuring that, now that
your application has gone

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:35.539
from being a monolith
to being composed

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:37.549
of, say, 100 separate
services, how

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:39.750
do you ensure that each
of those is secure?

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.140
So three big things for Istio--

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:47.680
observability,
agility, and security.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:48.870
How does it do that?

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:50.453
I won't go through
all the components,

00:08:50.453 --> 00:08:52.770
but I will tell you
that the key here

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:56.420
is through the use
of a sidecar proxy.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.190
Sidecar proxy is simply a
proxy that gets deployed

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:01.210
alongside your application.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:03.030
If you're doing
this in a VM, you've

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:05.700
got to make sure that that
proxy gets installed on the VM.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:07.590
But if you're using
Kubernetes, Kubernetes

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:10.200
has great features that allow
that to happen transparently.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.090
So whenever you
deploy a pod, you

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:14.520
put whatever you want
in your containers,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.790
Kubernetes can automatically
deploy the sidecar

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:19.380
alongside your pod.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:23.880
And all traffic into
and out of your service

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:25.320
goes through that--

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:26.721
goes through that proxy.

00:09:26.721 --> 00:09:28.470
And that means it can
make smart decisions

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:30.750
about how to route that call.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:34.600
And we'll get into some details
later about what that means.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:37.380
But also as a call comes
in, can make decisions about

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.530
whether or not that call
should be allowed in.

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:43.350
And it does all that,
again, transparently.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:45.480
Your services can be
communicating via whatever

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:46.820
protocol they want.

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.450
HTTP for most of you.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:50.130
GRPC for some of you.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:51.630
Could be just a TCP connection.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.220
It doesn't matter to us.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:56.790
All the traffic goes in and out,
and by interposing that proxy

00:09:56.790 --> 00:10:00.054
in there, we get to add
in some functionality.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:07.160
This project is something that
we started about 18 months ago,

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:11.330
and we have been working with
some partners the whole time.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:14.630
IBM and Lyft both started from
the very beginning with us.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:16.820
We use Lyft's envoy proxy.

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:21.160
Super, super performant,
small footprint proxy

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:24.650
that we really like and is
really getting great adoption

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:26.210
across the industry.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:28.940
But we're also working with
a bunch of other pretty heavy

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.500
hitters in the industry.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.910
Red Hat is working on this.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:34.440
VMware has done a
bunch of work on it.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:35.690
We're working with Cisco.

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:37.100
And so we're really not--

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:40.580
we're really trying to
get a consortium of people

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.491
from around the
industry to say, hey,

00:10:42.491 --> 00:10:43.740
this is a good way to do this.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.740
This is a good way to
solve this problem.

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:48.760
And we're getting as many
companies as we can behind it.

00:10:50.784 --> 00:10:52.200
NORI HEIKKINEN:
So the first point

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:54.807
Dan mentioned a couple of
slides ago was observability.

00:10:54.807 --> 00:10:56.640
So I'm going to give
you a little background

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:58.380
about observability
best practices

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:01.610
and then how we've
implemented those at Google.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:03.900
So the first thing is
observability begins

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.340
with monitoring.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:10.350
Now, I admit that calling
monitoring an SRE best practice

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:11.640
is a bit of an exaggeration.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:13.920
It's very fundamental.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:15.570
A brief story about this.

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:18.540
So when Mikey Dickerson,
who's a former Google SRE,

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.580
went out to DC as
part of the tech surge

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.980
to help fix healthcare.gov
a few years ago,

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.884
he found that they
had no monitoring.

00:11:24.884 --> 00:11:27.300
Well, correction, they actually
did have a very basic form

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:29.550
of monitoring, it
was a TV on the wall

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:31.370
and it was playing CNN.

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:33.600
And whenever CNN reported--
this is no joke--

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.352
whenever CNN reported that
healthcare.gov was down,

00:11:36.352 --> 00:11:37.560
they knew they had a problem.

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.053
They should probably go fix it.

00:11:39.053 --> 00:11:40.620
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:42.730
DANIEL CIRULI: Anyone
else use CNN as a service?

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:43.941
They're the only ones?

00:11:43.941 --> 00:11:46.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.770
NORI HEIKKINEN: I'll
tell you how to fix that.

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:52.470
Mikey, perhaps, got
a little exasperated

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:55.000
and drew them a
hierarchy of needs.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.240
He called it a
"reliability hierarchy."

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.460
And this has become known
as the Dickerson Hierarchy

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:00.957
of Reliability since then.

00:12:00.957 --> 00:12:02.790
Modeled, of course,
after Maslow's Hierarchy

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:05.760
of Needs of physiological
safety first

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:07.830
and then on up through
friendship and things

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:09.540
like that.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.520
In the Dickerson
Hierarchy of Reliability,

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:14.100
you have monitoring
right at the bottom

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.890
because having CNN on the
wall is not an acceptable form

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:17.947
of monitoring.

00:12:17.947 --> 00:12:20.280
And so in this pyramid that
I'm going to show right now,

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.290
I've sort of extrapolated
monitoring out

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:26.960
into its own pyramid with
its sub building blocks.

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:29.170
What should you monitor?

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:34.030
At Google and over the course of
developing many best practices

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:36.700
over the years, we've
decided that we have

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:38.260
at least four golden signals.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:40.090
And these are four
things that if you

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:43.540
monitor it will give you a
really good, basic insight

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:45.660
into how your system is doing.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.760
And they are, in
this order, traffic.

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.800
How many requests are
coming into your system?

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:54.190
This is commonly known as
RPS, Requests Per Second.

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:57.400
At Google, we still call them
QPS for most of our systems

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:00.490
because every request is
a web search query, right?

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:02.830
That sort of stuck around.

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:04.330
But this is your
building block here

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:07.930
is, how much traffic
are you serving?

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:09.490
Beyond this is errors.

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:11.860
Of that traffic
that you're serving,

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:13.506
are you serving it successfully?

00:13:13.506 --> 00:13:15.130
Are you serving any
of it successfully?

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:17.450
Are you saying all
of it successfully?

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:20.170
You need to be monitoring
not only the requests that

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:22.390
come into your system but
also the percentage of them

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:23.089
that fail.

00:13:23.089 --> 00:13:24.130
And I do mean percentage.

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:27.100
You have to start with traffic,
so you have a denominator here.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.740
But if you know that you
have 1,000 errors per second

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:32.480
that you're serving, that's
probably a bad thing.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:35.080
But if 1,000 requests
per second, or QPS,

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:38.590
is actually 0.001% of
your overall traffic,

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:42.280
that might be an acceptable-ish
amount of errors.

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:45.620
So, this is a rate here.

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:47.000
The next one is latency.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.420
And we'll find that, as
we go up this pyramid,

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:52.990
we're sort of moving out of
the realm of, we need oxygen

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:54.700
and we need water.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:57.490
And more into things
like, we need friendship.

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:59.690
And latency is a
really good sort

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:03.700
of friendship analogy in
this monitoring pyramid here.

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:06.220
Latency is not
quite as fundamental

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:08.050
as traffic and
errors, but you still

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:11.800
do need to be monitoring
latency to understand

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.110
the overall performance
of your system.

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:17.950
Users expect apps to be
super fast these days.

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:20.140
And in this highly
networked world,

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:22.030
most of that budget
for latency is

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:23.860
going to come out of your code.

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:26.020
And so you do want to
be measuring latency.

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:29.020
An interesting note on
how to do this here.

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:31.090
Don't just do this as
a straight average.

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:35.860
Latency, if you took the average
of all the latency of all

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.110
of the requests coming into
and out of your system,

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:40.481
you would end up [INAUDIBLE]
some important points.

00:14:40.481 --> 00:14:42.730
You wouldn't get a sense of
what the common experience

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:45.400
of your app is because you
might have some requests way out

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:47.890
at your long tail, which
for various reasons,

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:50.860
might be taking a very
long time to complete.

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:53.290
And these could be for
legitimate reasons or reasons

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.340
that you want to look
into a little more deeply.

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:57.464
But either way, it's really
good to break that out.

00:14:57.464 --> 00:14:59.800
And so when we talk about
monitoring latency at Google,

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:02.950
we talk about it in terms
of, generally, three numbers.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:04.990
We talk about the
50th percentile,

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:06.160
or the median latency.

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.430
We often refer to that as P50.

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:10.960
And then the 90th
percentile, P90.

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:12.430
And then P99 as well.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:15.020
And taking those three
lines as separate,

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:16.840
you usually see them--

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:20.020
P50 is pretty steady
state for your system.

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:22.900
P90 is probably
generally a little higher

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:26.300
but should still be within
the realm of normal.

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.227
And P99 is a really interesting
signal to split out here.

00:15:29.227 --> 00:15:31.060
So, I think that's just
an important concept

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:33.280
to mention that latency
as an average is not

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:35.850
going to get you very far.

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:37.780
And finally, saturation.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.820
This is a metric
that is even fuzzier,

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:44.140
and this one is up to you
to define a little more

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:45.730
than the other three.

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:49.390
Saturation is the idea of, at
what point is your service just

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:50.440
going to fall over?

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:52.850
If you had no load
shedding, no auto scaling

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:55.060
enabled, nothing like that.

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:58.470
You need to know about the
capacity of your service.

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:00.370
This could be as simple
as CPU utilization.

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:03.100
It's a very common saturation
metric out of the box.

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:05.380
But it also might be that,
based on your knowledge

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:07.240
of your own system,
it's actually

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:10.360
a much more complicated
metric that you calculate

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:14.830
and that you can export a
variable based on this one.

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:16.810
But what you want to
do with saturation

00:16:16.810 --> 00:16:20.810
is feed that into
things I mentioned,

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:21.827
such as auto scaling.

00:16:21.827 --> 00:16:24.160
And it can be a signal to
tell the system that it's time

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:26.860
to auto scale up
and add more tasks.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.320
It can be a signal
to load shedding.

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:31.870
To say that the system is full,
we can't auto scale any more,

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:33.640
and we want to start
dropping queries.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:36.670
It could just be a white
box signal to page a human

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:38.840
and say, please
come add more jobs.

00:16:38.840 --> 00:16:41.630
We're about to fall over, the
system is almost at capacity.

00:16:41.630 --> 00:16:43.750
But no matter how
you treat it, these--

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:46.600
saturation is the fourth
of these golden signals

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:50.170
that, taken together, form a
pretty good idea of the health

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:51.220
of your overall system.

00:16:53.794 --> 00:16:55.960
So in the old days at Google,
whenever a new service

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:57.392
wanted to launch,
they would have

00:16:57.392 --> 00:16:59.600
to basically write their
own monitoring from scratch.

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:01.450
And it wasn't quite
as dire as that.

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:03.580
I mean, there was
a standard library.

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:06.430
Each service would include
the standard library

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:08.950
and then export a standard
set of variables, requests

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:11.560
for seconds, errors, et cetera.

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:14.859
But then, the manual part was
that each new service owner

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:18.339
had to manually aggregate
these and define the monitoring

00:17:18.339 --> 00:17:20.560
configuration to
aggregate these all up.

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:23.500
And then define, what were the
dashboards going to look like.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.420
It was a very tedious process.

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:26.530
Trust me, I did it once.

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:28.960
I've got the badge
to prove it, but I

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:30.910
don't want to do it again.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.580
And so what's cool these days is
that when new services launch,

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:36.070
or really when you turn up
any job in our production

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:38.920
environment, you get
this for free these days.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:40.900
Now we use a sidecar proxy.

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:43.330
And the sidecar proxy is
automatically collecting

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:44.860
the metrics that I mentioned.

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:47.380
It's collecting the golden
signals and a fair number

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:49.090
of other ones besides.

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:51.610
And it automatically puts
these into a dashboard for you.

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:57.890
So these days, when we have
this automatic aggregation--

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:00.700
this is a screenshot of the
dashboard that you'll see.

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:03.100
We just picked an arbitrary
small service here.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:05.800
But you can see that these are
three basic graphs that just

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:08.170
list out the golden signals.

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:11.890
This-- no work was done on
the part of the service owner

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:13.300
to get these graphs.

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:15.820
This is observability for free.

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:18.800
And this has been an incredibly
powerful concept at Google.

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:22.250
Not only does it remove the
need for each individual service

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:25.220
owner to redefine their
monitoring from scratch,

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:27.170
but it also means
that, when you want

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:29.750
to look into the performance
of somebody else's system

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:32.280
within our organization,
you can do that.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:34.280
And so in many cases--

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:37.490
again, in the old days, we would
maintain these lists of links

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:39.650
to the various
monitoring dashboards

00:18:39.650 --> 00:18:41.072
and these other
services, and you

00:18:41.072 --> 00:18:42.530
hope that then you
remember to find

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:44.420
where your page of
aggregated links is.

00:18:44.420 --> 00:18:47.450
And that nobody else moved their
dashboards or they're not down.

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:48.830
It was a bit of a nightmare.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:52.640
And these days, if you
have a service that

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:55.100
has a dependency on
some other service,

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:56.720
you suspect the
dependency may be

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:59.780
at fault in some issue
you're currently debugging,

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:02.540
you don't have to find
your bookmarked link.

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:05.060
You can just go to our
central automatic monitoring

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:07.760
dashboard, type in
their service name,

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:09.170
and view their dashboards.

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:09.740
It's so cool.

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:11.949
Of course, these other
services can go in and tweak

00:19:11.949 --> 00:19:12.990
these metrics themselves.

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:14.780
They can make custom dashboards.

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:16.220
They can make it prettier.

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:19.580
But what you're seeing here is
that out of the box, at Google,

00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:22.910
you just get
dashboards for free.

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:25.820
These are a couple of
zoom-in dashboards here.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.580
This is the top one you see,
this says requests in errors.

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:31.130
There's three lines on here
and so you-- we actually

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:33.920
almost always will graph
the request per second

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:36.350
against errors per
second there in two types

00:19:36.350 --> 00:19:38.210
of errors in this one.

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:40.270
We also have the
internal error ratio,

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:43.880
which is the second piece
of that monitoring pyramid.

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:44.470
And latency.

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:46.220
Again, you'll notice
on the latency graph,

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:47.630
there's those three lines.

00:19:47.630 --> 00:19:50.060
And the red one is P50 latency.

00:19:50.060 --> 00:19:52.590
Pretty normal
realm for that one.

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:54.832
The next one up is P90 latency.

00:19:54.832 --> 00:19:56.540
And that's still within
the normal realm.

00:19:56.540 --> 00:19:59.140
You can see in this particular
graph, it's about double

00:19:59.140 --> 00:20:01.407
of what the P50 latency is.

00:20:01.407 --> 00:20:03.740
That may or may not be expected
based on the performance

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:04.790
of your system.

00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:07.137
But then P99 latency,
I think this perfectly

00:20:07.137 --> 00:20:09.470
illustrates why it's really
important to split these out

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:12.492
into their own bands so you can
reason about them separately.

00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:18.510
The other part of observability
as a complement to the metrics

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:20.670
and the dashboards, is logging.

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:24.030
When you're debugging an issue,
dashboards are a great start,

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:26.670
and often they'll tell
you what you need to know.

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:29.190
Sometimes you'll
see a steep drop-off

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:31.380
on the number of running
tasks or a steep uptick

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:34.380
in the number of queries per
second coming to your system.

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:35.890
Those are great places to start.

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:38.181
But occasionally, you'll get
into a really gnarly issue

00:20:38.181 --> 00:20:40.470
where you need to dig
into exactly what happened

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:42.690
at a certain period of time.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:47.190
At Google, our systems
generate huge volume of logs.

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:50.630
We're doing huge volume
of requests at all times.

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:52.020
We basically log everything.

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:53.980
We have reasonable
retention periods.

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:55.676
But for a finite
period of time, when

00:20:55.676 --> 00:20:58.050
you're trying to understand
what happened in your system,

00:20:58.050 --> 00:21:00.930
you can go back and
dig through these logs

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:04.550
to try to figure out, in
precise detail, what happened.

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:06.180
But the only reason
this works at all

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:10.710
is that we have tools to help
us slice and dice this data.

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:15.234
Petabytes of logs are useless
if all you have is grep.

00:21:15.234 --> 00:21:17.430
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:18.450
And there was a groan.

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:21.090
Yeah, that's how I
feel about that one.

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:23.970
Dremel is our internal
SQL like interface

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:25.956
over these huge volumes of logs.

00:21:25.956 --> 00:21:27.330
It lets us drill
down into what's

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:29.121
happening at the precise
moment we need it.

00:21:29.121 --> 00:21:31.350
And it's a much
more powerful tool

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:33.870
than simply trying to open
these up in your editor

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:37.920
and muck around to
try to distill down.

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:41.370
I have been at Google long
enough that it turns out

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:44.160
that Dremel is such
a power tool for me

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:47.400
that it took me many years,
more than I'd like to admit,

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:49.620
of being an SRE before
somebody actually pointed out

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:53.367
to me that a dremel is
a real, physical thing.

00:21:53.367 --> 00:21:55.155
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:56.575 --> 00:21:57.450
DANIEL CIRULI: Great.

00:21:57.450 --> 00:22:01.250
So thank you, Nori,
for that and explaining

00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:04.110
what are these things that
we feel you need to have.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:05.910
The tools in your
arsenal that you

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:08.220
need if you want to be an SRE.

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:11.550
And now I want to talk a little
bit about how Istio gives you

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:12.910
some of those tools.

00:22:12.910 --> 00:22:15.900
Sure, I can drive, I suppose.

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:17.980
Didn't like me before.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:22.910
Again, emphasizing what I
said about a service mesh.

00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:25.140
You can get observability today.

00:22:25.140 --> 00:22:26.960
All of you are doing
some monitoring.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:29.580
No one is really
using CNN, right?

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:32.280
But what tends to happen
is, different teams

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:33.970
solve this in different ways.

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:35.790
And if you're
running operations,

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:37.830
depending on which team
developed a service,

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:39.990
you might have a
different set of metrics.

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:41.880
Some things might be
gathered from logs.

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:45.750
Other people are somehow
sending telemetry somewhere.

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:48.030
Other things they would
like to use the same metrics

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:49.800
that that team has,
but they're developing

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:52.050
in a different language
and the client library just

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:54.320
doesn't work as well in Node
as it does in Java, say.

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:58.860
So with Istio, we want to
give a standard set of metrics

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:00.750
without doing any integration.

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:05.130
Without language specific
client libraries.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:08.220
And out of the box, we give
you most of the golden signals.

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:09.600
Traffic, errors, and latency.

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:13.170
The things that Nori said, you
can't really run a service well

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:14.320
without these metrics.

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:15.330
We give you that out of the box.

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:17.670
So every service that you're
deploying into your service

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:19.140
mesh, you get that
out of the box.

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:22.080
Now, as she said, saturation
is different for every service.

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:23.850
In some cases, it might be CPU.

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:25.900
In some cases, it might be RAM.

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:28.960
Other ones, it might be a number
of operations going to disk.

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:30.960
You'll have to figure
that out for your service.

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:34.560
But for most of these, out
of the box, we'll gather them

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:38.830
and we'll send them to the
location of your choosing.

00:23:38.830 --> 00:23:40.850
And to do that we use
a component in Istio

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:42.030
called Mixer.

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:44.770
Mixer is a runtime component
we deploy into your Kubernetes

00:23:44.770 --> 00:23:45.770
cluster.

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:48.130
And it has a very
pluggable architecture.

00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:49.960
All of these sidecar
proxies, when

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:52.410
they receive requests,
when they return requests,

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:55.180
they are sending all
this telemetry to Mixer.

00:23:55.180 --> 00:23:58.150
Mixer, then, can plug into
the system of your choice.

00:23:58.150 --> 00:23:59.770
Maybe you're using Prometheus.

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:01.840
Maybe you just run an Influx DB.

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:03.856
Maybe you're using
external vendors.

00:24:03.856 --> 00:24:05.230
People are writing
these already,

00:24:05.230 --> 00:24:06.771
and certainly we
have a fantastic one

00:24:06.771 --> 00:24:08.650
for our Stackdriver tool.

00:24:08.650 --> 00:24:11.410
Mixer is the component that--
we won't get into policy today,

00:24:11.410 --> 00:24:15.440
but in addition to
enforcing policy,

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:18.419
it also gathers all the
telemetry and sends it out.

00:24:18.419 --> 00:24:19.960
And of course, we
think a great place

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:22.540
to send this is Stackdriver.

00:24:22.540 --> 00:24:25.840
This is in Alpha in
Stackdriver today,

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.590
and it's a fantastic
view into not just

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:30.340
what's going on with
a particular service,

00:24:30.340 --> 00:24:32.860
but how these services
are interoperating.

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:35.530
This is a dependency graph,
and it builds this graph

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:38.350
depending on the actual traffic
observed in your system.

00:24:38.350 --> 00:24:40.030
This is a really big deal.

00:24:40.030 --> 00:24:42.340
When someone joins
your team tomorrow,

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:44.980
I guarantee you someone is
going to go to a whiteboard

00:24:44.980 --> 00:24:48.100
and draw a picture of what
all your services are.

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:50.455
And I can also guarantee,
it's probably wrong.

00:24:50.455 --> 00:24:52.147
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:52.147 --> 00:24:53.980
I know this from when
we got this at Google.

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:55.270
People don't know
their dependencies.

00:24:55.270 --> 00:24:56.830
They don't know who's
dependent on them.

00:24:56.830 --> 00:24:58.270
This actually knows,
and it can tell you.

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:00.430
And it can tell you not just
how much traffic you're getting,

00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:02.950
but how much traffic you're
getting from each client.

00:25:02.950 --> 00:25:04.450
When things are
running slowly, this

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:06.380
can help you root
it out very quickly.

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:08.230
And as you can see
on the left there,

00:25:08.230 --> 00:25:10.470
you get the golden signals
for every one of these.

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:12.220
You get your traffic,
you get your errors,

00:25:12.220 --> 00:25:13.345
and you get your latency.

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:17.810
We collect all your logs, too.

00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:20.300
And I sometimes talk to
customers and they ask,

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:24.090
what percentage of our
requests should be logged?

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:27.470
And the answer is easy,
it's a triple-digit number.

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:28.610
You log all your requests.

00:25:28.610 --> 00:25:29.780
You don't have to
keep them forever.

00:25:29.780 --> 00:25:31.520
But when something
goes wrong, you never

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:33.640
know which request was going
to make that thing go wrong.

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:35.556
And if you weren't
logging, you're in trouble.

00:25:35.556 --> 00:25:38.090
But as Nori said, lots of logs
are difficult to deal with

00:25:38.090 --> 00:25:40.990
and almost unusable unless
you have good tools.

00:25:40.990 --> 00:25:42.950
Stackdriver logs.

00:25:42.950 --> 00:25:44.490
It's got a great logs viewer.

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:46.880
But even better, you can
pipe it into BigQuery.

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:49.160
And in BigQuery, it's Dremel.

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:51.890
You can write great SQL
queries across those

00:25:51.890 --> 00:25:53.660
logs to do analytics.

00:25:53.660 --> 00:25:56.460
You can figure out what went
wrong in a particular event.

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:58.670
And you know, big
analytics things.

00:25:58.670 --> 00:26:01.742
It's a really,
really powerful tool.

00:26:01.742 --> 00:26:03.950
I didn't get into tracing,
but Istio's also gathering

00:26:03.950 --> 00:26:06.900
tracing for you as well.

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:09.489
Now talk about SLIs.

00:26:09.489 --> 00:26:11.780
NORI HEIKKINEN: So once you're
monitoring your service,

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:14.300
you can use these signals to
talk about health at a service

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:14.930
level.

00:26:14.930 --> 00:26:16.457
And then also to
help you plan how

00:26:16.457 --> 00:26:18.290
you're going to react
if those signals start

00:26:18.290 --> 00:26:20.500
to dip a little bit.

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:24.000
There's lots of TLAs in this
space, please bear with me.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:27.260
An SLI, this is
metrics, essentially.

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:29.840
This is, basically,
the golden signals

00:26:29.840 --> 00:26:31.350
are a great place to start.

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:34.250
They are just a sense
of what's happening

00:26:34.250 --> 00:26:38.400
in your system on a
quantifiable basis.

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:41.460
Also, I should note that
these are often the things

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:43.800
that you define your alerts on.

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:46.181
Alerting is something I could
give its own 50-minute talk

00:26:46.181 --> 00:26:46.680
about.

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:49.140
But very briefly, if you
alert on the golden signals,

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:51.640
that's a great place to start.

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:54.160
SLO uses the SLIs.

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:55.840
These are targets
for these metrics.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:58.870
They're either target
values or target ranges.

00:26:58.870 --> 00:27:00.430
Pro tip here.

00:27:00.430 --> 00:27:05.380
100% is not a good SLO.

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:09.580
Typical availability ranges are
measured in number of nines.

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:11.590
This is, again, very
service dependent.

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:13.900
It may be two nines, depending--

00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:17.340
99%, depending on what your
service can reasonably achieve.

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:20.170
Or it might be five or six
nines, a very highly available

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:21.040
service.

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:24.250
But again, 100% is just
never going to happen.

00:27:24.250 --> 00:27:27.490
One of the fundamental tenets
of engineering for reliability

00:27:27.490 --> 00:27:28.927
is, you have to
plan for failure.

00:27:28.927 --> 00:27:30.010
Things are going to break.

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:31.176
Machines are going to break.

00:27:31.176 --> 00:27:32.500
VMs are going to break.

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:34.390
The network is going to go down.

00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:35.710
There will be failures.

00:27:35.710 --> 00:27:39.730
And so assuming, or just hoping
that they will never happen

00:27:39.730 --> 00:27:43.270
is, as we say in
SRE, not a strategy.

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:45.890
So set a really high service--

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:47.500
service level objective.

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:51.950
But measure it in nines
and don't use 100% here.

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:54.560
An SLA is a legal agreement.

00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:58.240
This is basically a
threshold after which

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:01.567
the-- you the customer
or your customers,

00:28:01.567 --> 00:28:03.650
depending on which direction
you're talking about,

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:07.250
get remunerations
or other things

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:09.945
coming back to them in
breach of the contract.

00:28:09.945 --> 00:28:11.570
So when we're talking
about reliability

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:13.610
from the engineering
standpoint, very often

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:16.432
internally we're
speaking of SLIs and SLOs

00:28:16.432 --> 00:28:18.140
because those are the
things that we have

00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:19.834
the most direct control over.

00:28:19.834 --> 00:28:22.250
Choosing what to measure and
then choosing what thresholds

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:23.590
are actually achievable.

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:26.720
And we leave SLAs to the
more business and legal end

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:27.780
of the company.

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:33.000
Another way of saying
SLO is by, instead

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:35.760
of looking at the area
under that target threshold,

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:37.530
looking at the area above.

00:28:37.530 --> 00:28:39.540
And that's your error budget.

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:42.240
I love this concept.

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:44.400
People can think of
it, and typically do,

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:46.254
very productively
as a constraint.

00:28:46.254 --> 00:28:47.670
And I think that's
great, and I'll

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:49.987
talk through why I
like that in a minute.

00:28:49.987 --> 00:28:51.820
But I think it's also
important to remember,

00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:55.370
the flip side of that is that
it can be permissive as well.

00:28:55.370 --> 00:28:56.440
So the constraint side.

00:28:56.440 --> 00:28:58.950
This is the typical way to
think about an error budget.

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:03.120
You set your SLOs, you
monitor, you define alerts.

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:06.210
You have those alerts
paging humans or, you know,

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:07.920
maybe feeding back
into automated systems

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:09.360
to take action.

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:12.930
And then you take some sort
of action at the system level

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:14.670
to increase reliability
if you dip down

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:15.900
below your error budget.

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:17.930
Again, this could be
it's own 50-minute talk.

00:29:17.930 --> 00:29:19.770
But very briefly,
a typical response

00:29:19.770 --> 00:29:22.860
to going out of error
budget is to freeze.

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:25.920
Freezes are basically
the idea, if you

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:28.770
stop pushing change
live to your system,

00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:30.990
change, be it in the
form of a new release

00:29:30.990 --> 00:29:35.190
candidate of your binary or
binaries or, even more, config,

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:37.110
we recognize that
all of these things

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:39.100
are vectors of instability.

00:29:39.100 --> 00:29:41.130
And so you need to
manage that instability.

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:43.140
Typically, the way you
do this is you just

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:46.380
stop pushing until you're
back within your error budget

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:47.260
to recover that.

00:29:47.260 --> 00:29:48.970
And the idea here is twofold.

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:51.330
One, it gives your
customers or your users

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:53.700
a greater period of
stability because you just

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:58.320
experienced an outage, or maybe
a very slow trickle of outages,

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:01.802
and you'd like to have them have
overall a very good experience.

00:30:01.802 --> 00:30:03.510
The other idea here
is that typically you

00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:05.176
can redirect the
engineering effort that

00:30:05.176 --> 00:30:08.670
would have been going into new
features into reliability work.

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:13.650
A flip side of this
is the way in which

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:15.780
an error budget is permissive.

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:17.677
As a former manager
of mine used to say,

00:30:17.677 --> 00:30:20.010
what good are all those nines
if you can't take them out

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:21.260
for a spin every now and then?

00:30:21.260 --> 00:30:23.010
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:23.010 --> 00:30:25.620
But like, I did say it
jokingly and so did he,

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:28.500
but what I like about this
is it actually gives you

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:33.100
a good sense of when it is a
good time to take extra risks.

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:36.120
Is it a good time right before
your big annual conference?

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:37.000
Probably not.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:40.140
But, if you're-- the week
after, if you are well within

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:43.110
your error budget and your
engineers have been asking

00:30:43.110 --> 00:30:45.870
to try out a crazy new feature
that's very well tested

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:48.750
but does carry a
greater degree of risk,

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:50.130
you have a budget to spend.

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:53.170
That's why I like to
think of it as a budget.

00:30:53.170 --> 00:30:54.920
DANIEL CIRULI: If
you've ever been waiting

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:56.840
for a new feature from
some Google product

00:30:56.840 --> 00:31:01.521
and it seemed to get delayed
maybe a month later than you

00:31:01.521 --> 00:31:03.770
wanted it, it may be that
that team ran out of budget.

00:31:03.770 --> 00:31:06.080
And they weren't permitted
to launch that feature.

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:08.984
It really does affect
how we launch things.

00:31:08.984 --> 00:31:10.400
So now I want to
talk a little bit

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:12.710
about one of the
very cool things that

00:31:12.710 --> 00:31:16.520
isn't specific to Istio
alone, but it's something

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:19.460
that you get when you combine
Istio and Stackdriver.

00:31:19.460 --> 00:31:21.940
And that's the
ability to define--

00:31:21.940 --> 00:31:25.400
to monitor your SLIs
and define SLOs.

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:27.560
So I mentioned
this before, but we

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:32.390
are shipping today in open
source an adapter with Istio

00:31:32.390 --> 00:31:34.580
to send your metrics
to Stackdriver.

00:31:34.580 --> 00:31:37.100
That includes your traces,
it includes your logs,

00:31:37.100 --> 00:31:39.820
but of course the telemetry,
the things that were powering

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:41.667
that graph I showed you before.

00:31:41.667 --> 00:31:43.250
Well, they've got a
nifty new feature.

00:31:43.250 --> 00:31:47.510
And their nifty new
feature is the ability

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:50.529
to set service level objectives.

00:31:50.529 --> 00:31:52.070
And what's awesome
is because they're

00:31:52.070 --> 00:31:57.080
getting the metrics
from your services,

00:31:57.080 --> 00:32:00.830
you can now set SLOs,
Service Level Objectives,

00:32:00.830 --> 00:32:02.720
on the golden signals.

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:06.060
It seems like an obvious thing,
but it's a fantastic thing.

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:09.560
I literally know customers
who alert because they

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:11.570
have their logs, they
scrape their logs,

00:32:11.570 --> 00:32:13.700
they send their logs
to a third party vendor

00:32:13.700 --> 00:32:17.870
where they can write a grep,
run that grep every say, 5 or 15

00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:20.480
minutes, and then, if they have
too many errors, do an alert.

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:22.310
Who does something like that?

00:32:22.310 --> 00:32:24.227
Some of you are not being
completely truthful.

00:32:24.227 --> 00:32:25.226
I know that's happening.

00:32:25.226 --> 00:32:25.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:30.322
It's really, really crit-- like,
you might as well turn on CNN.

00:32:30.322 --> 00:32:32.030
This is the way you
should be doing this.

00:32:32.030 --> 00:32:33.988
Have something that's
gathering your telemetry,

00:32:33.988 --> 00:32:35.169
set an [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:35.169 --> 00:32:37.460
And say, hey I want to make
sure that we are available,

00:32:37.460 --> 00:32:43.550
that we're returning our
requests successfully.

00:32:43.550 --> 00:32:44.649
Say, every week.

00:32:44.649 --> 00:32:46.940
You might do it per month,
you might do it per quarter.

00:32:46.940 --> 00:32:53.120
And say I want that to be at
a certain range, 91%, 92%.

00:32:53.120 --> 00:32:56.240
Now you set this objective
within Stackdriver,

00:32:56.240 --> 00:33:00.020
and Stackdriver will alert you
if you get out of compliance.

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:03.020
Now, you might not set
this where your SLA is.

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:04.550
If you're telling
your customers,

00:33:04.550 --> 00:33:07.994
you know, 99.9, you might
set this a little bit higher

00:33:07.994 --> 00:33:09.910
so that you get alerted
before your customer--

00:33:09.910 --> 00:33:11.390
before you're out of compliance.

00:33:11.390 --> 00:33:15.240
But then they will let you
monitor this, they'll show you,

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:19.460
here's how you're doing
against your goal.

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:22.000
And you can see sometimes I'm
above, sometimes I'm below.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:25.610
But what's important, they'll
show you your budget as well.

00:33:25.610 --> 00:33:27.990
In the aggregate, are we
doing OK, or are we not?

00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:30.160
Are we positive,
or are we negative?

00:33:30.160 --> 00:33:33.290
So it's a really fantastic tool.

00:33:33.290 --> 00:33:35.270
It's taking some
very basic concepts.

00:33:35.270 --> 00:33:38.277
If you've read the SRE book,
you will have read about this.

00:33:38.277 --> 00:33:40.610
And you may have thought,
wow, error budgets sound cool.

00:33:40.610 --> 00:33:42.320
How would I implement that?

00:33:42.320 --> 00:33:43.960
The good news is
that they just did

00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:46.800
and that you can take
advantage of this.

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:48.387
And now back to you.

00:33:48.387 --> 00:33:50.220
NORI HEIKKINEN: So
another SRE best practice

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:51.900
is that of canary releases.

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:54.810
And we do it so much
we call it canarying.

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:57.090
So as I mentioned when I
was talking about freezes,

00:33:57.090 --> 00:34:00.060
new releases commonly
cause instability.

00:34:00.060 --> 00:34:02.190
So you need to manage
that instability.

00:34:02.190 --> 00:34:06.480
Canary jobs are small jobs that
are basically identical to all

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:09.389
of the other workloads you
have running in production,

00:34:09.389 --> 00:34:11.800
but they're configured
to get traffic early.

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:15.630
And they also, importantly,
take a very small fraction

00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:16.650
of your traffic.

00:34:16.650 --> 00:34:18.780
And so the same concept
applies as the canary

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:19.530
in the mine shaft.

00:34:19.530 --> 00:34:21.154
You send the canary
down the mine shaft

00:34:21.154 --> 00:34:24.239
first so that, if toxic fumes
have built up down there,

00:34:24.239 --> 00:34:27.540
the canary keels over instead
of all of the mining team

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:28.380
all at once.

00:34:28.380 --> 00:34:30.040
Same concept applies here.

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:33.409
If you send a binary down
into the mine shaft--

00:34:33.409 --> 00:34:35.130
this analogy is breaking down--

00:34:35.130 --> 00:34:38.460
and it falls over, you know
then to not immediately

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:41.489
continue your rollout to all
of production all at once,

00:34:41.489 --> 00:34:45.239
creating a huge outage.

00:34:45.239 --> 00:34:49.530
A couple points about how
to do canarying effectively.

00:34:49.530 --> 00:34:52.469
One is you need to
monitor the canary.

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:55.760
Because this is, remember,
running a very small percentage

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:57.930
of your traffic-- it might
be 1% of your traffic,

00:34:57.930 --> 00:35:01.850
it might be even less than that
depending on your service--

00:35:01.850 --> 00:35:04.106
you need to monitor
it independently.

00:35:04.106 --> 00:35:05.730
If you just fold it
into the monitoring

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:07.612
for your overall
service, the error

00:35:07.612 --> 00:35:09.570
is coming forward, even
if the canary is loudly

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:11.550
cheeping in its
death throes, are

00:35:11.550 --> 00:35:13.440
going to get folded
in to the rest

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:15.660
of your overall
aggregate alerting.

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:17.640
And very likely, you
won't hear about it.

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:20.360
So you need specific
alerting on your canary tasks

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:21.990
or your canary jobs.

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:26.370
And they need to be causing
somebody to take an action.

00:35:26.370 --> 00:35:28.860
Either that's the automated
rollout server automatically

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:31.980
rolls back or a human hits
the pause button or something.

00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:34.200
But either way, you
need to monitor these

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:36.400
independently so that you
can catch things early,

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:38.087
which is the whole point.

00:35:38.087 --> 00:35:40.170
And the other thing is,
you need to pace yourself.

00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:43.230
Your service may go
through peaks and valleys.

00:35:43.230 --> 00:35:46.230
In web search, back in
the day, probably still,

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:48.810
we had a nice diurnal curve.

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:51.720
And your service may
have different traffic

00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:52.890
characteristics.

00:35:52.890 --> 00:35:55.350
Also depending on the
deployment of your service,

00:35:55.350 --> 00:35:57.750
the various locations may
receive a different traffic

00:35:57.750 --> 00:35:58.760
mix.

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:02.160
And so you ideally want to have
canary jobs in each region,

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:04.920
or in each place, or in
each configuration that

00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:08.760
has a unique perspective
on your service just

00:36:08.760 --> 00:36:10.509
to get the best signal you can.

00:36:10.509 --> 00:36:12.300
And so you want to go
slowly here, whatever

00:36:12.300 --> 00:36:13.840
that means for your service.

00:36:13.840 --> 00:36:15.870
In the case of something
like web search,

00:36:15.870 --> 00:36:18.690
you might choose to do a
one-day canary period just

00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:21.510
to get the full diurnal
cycle and make sure

00:36:21.510 --> 00:36:23.880
that your canary really
sees all kinds of traffic.

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:27.900
In something where you have
a much more aggressive CICD

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:29.970
on like an every
half hour basis,

00:36:29.970 --> 00:36:32.460
the canary may only be
30 seconds or a minute

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:33.144
or something.

00:36:33.144 --> 00:36:34.560
But this is something
that you are

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:37.260
going to want to define
to be meaningfully

00:36:37.260 --> 00:36:38.640
slow for your service.

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:42.657
DANIEL CIRULI: Guess what?

00:36:42.657 --> 00:36:43.740
Istio helps with that too.

00:36:46.329 --> 00:36:48.870
I want to start by talking about
the problem with Kubernetes.

00:36:48.870 --> 00:36:51.000
And by the way, and Nori
did say, Dan, are you sure

00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:53.452
you want to title a slide
"The Problem with Kubernetes?"

00:36:53.452 --> 00:36:54.660
And I laughed and I said yes.

00:36:54.660 --> 00:36:56.460
Someone else said are you sure
you don't want to just call

00:36:56.460 --> 00:36:57.840
it "A problem with Kubernetes?"

00:36:57.840 --> 00:36:59.440
Because there might
be more than one.

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:02.070
But one problem
with Kubernetes is

00:37:02.070 --> 00:37:03.810
that Kubernetes,
it load balances.

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:07.290
If you've got a workload
that's running at enough scale

00:37:07.290 --> 00:37:09.090
that you need two
pods, it'll load

00:37:09.090 --> 00:37:10.410
balance between those two pods.

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:14.010
So if you-- version one,
you're doing enough QPS

00:37:14.010 --> 00:37:16.919
that it takes two to
satisfy that, it'll run two.

00:37:16.919 --> 00:37:18.960
Well, say you've got a
new version of that binary

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:19.950
that you deploy.

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:21.490
What is Kubernetes going to do?

00:37:21.490 --> 00:37:23.550
It'll bring up a new
version of that pod,

00:37:23.550 --> 00:37:27.150
and it will get an equal
share of the traffic.

00:37:27.150 --> 00:37:29.370
This is not a best
practice of Kubernetes.

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:31.500
If you need a 1%
rollout, you shouldn't

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:33.960
need to run 99 pods
of your other thing

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:35.632
in order to achieve
that 1% rollout.

00:37:35.632 --> 00:37:37.590
But that's literally what
you would have to do,

00:37:37.590 --> 00:37:40.330
and I've heard vendors talk
about things just like that.

00:37:40.330 --> 00:37:42.090
That's not the right
way to do things.

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.360
And she said, you want to
start with something small.

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:46.740
1%, for us sometimes it's
a tenth of a percent.

00:37:46.740 --> 00:37:48.240
And then you want
to go to, say, 10.

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:50.850
And then you want to
go to 20 or 50, right?

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:53.100
But you want to control
the pace of the rollout.

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:55.860
Not with the number of pods that
are running but with something

00:37:55.860 --> 00:37:57.870
a little bit more explicit.

00:37:57.870 --> 00:37:59.490
So we have a component
called Pilot.

00:37:59.490 --> 00:38:03.480
And Pilot is a component
that pushes route rules

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:05.940
to the sidecar proxies.

00:38:05.940 --> 00:38:09.030
And what this gives us is the
ability to do load balancing,

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:11.147
but importantly, it
enables us-- by the way,

00:38:11.147 --> 00:38:12.480
this is also running an ingress.

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:14.175
So for traffic coming from--

00:38:14.175 --> 00:38:17.610
in off the internet, it's also
going through an envoy proxy.

00:38:17.610 --> 00:38:20.349
And Pilot can tell
your services,

00:38:20.349 --> 00:38:22.140
whether they're the
ingress or whether it's

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:24.150
service-to-service
traffic, hey, there's

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:26.100
a new version of this
one, but you should only

00:38:26.100 --> 00:38:28.020
send 1% of the traffic to that.

00:38:28.020 --> 00:38:29.960
So it's easy.

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:32.340
YAML, and here you see it's
got some YAML that shows.

00:38:32.340 --> 00:38:35.880
We've got V1, I want that
to get a weight of 90.

00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:40.395
And I've got a V2, and I want
that to get a weight of 10.

00:38:40.395 --> 00:38:44.970
So in Kubernetes, it's
still running three pods,

00:38:44.970 --> 00:38:46.810
but they're not
getting equal shares.

00:38:46.810 --> 00:38:50.490
Instead, that version 2 is
getting the 10% you want.

00:38:50.490 --> 00:38:52.950
And of course, we are
gathering the metrics from it.

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:57.150
We're gathering the telemetry
so we can actually do

00:38:57.150 --> 00:38:58.450
the thing that you really want.

00:38:58.450 --> 00:39:01.740
Which is, you can look at your
telemetry for just that canary

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:04.020
and you can say, is this--

00:39:04.020 --> 00:39:05.100
is it compliant?

00:39:05.100 --> 00:39:07.110
Are the error ratios where
we expect them to be?

00:39:07.110 --> 00:39:09.068
Are the latencies where
they're expected to be?

00:39:09.068 --> 00:39:13.170
And if so, then we'll
go to that next phase.

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:15.330
Getting started with
Istio, we'd love for you

00:39:15.330 --> 00:39:17.027
to get started with Istio.

00:39:17.027 --> 00:39:19.110
As I said, we've been going
at it about 18 months.

00:39:19.110 --> 00:39:22.620
We are very, very close
to declaring it 1.0.

00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:25.530
That'll happen any day now.

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:26.590
We've done a lot of work.

00:39:26.590 --> 00:39:29.280
This is not a
half-baked project.

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:31.560
We have-- over 200
developers have

00:39:31.560 --> 00:39:32.700
contributed to the project.

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:34.350
We're very happy about that.

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:37.230
From a bunch of different
companies, as I said.

00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:39.270
We've got it in production.

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:42.100
eBay was on stage, I
think, talking about it.

00:39:42.100 --> 00:39:44.070
And sitting in the
front row here,

00:39:44.070 --> 00:39:46.260
we have a few
different customers.

00:39:46.260 --> 00:39:47.664
Auto Trader in the UK.

00:39:47.664 --> 00:39:49.830
We've got people who are
doing hundreds of thousands

00:39:49.830 --> 00:39:51.090
of requests a second.

00:39:51.090 --> 00:39:54.810
We have people who are doing
more than 1,000 services.

00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:57.750
These are all production
use cases I'm talking about.

00:39:57.750 --> 00:40:00.640
We've got adapters for
many monitoring systems,

00:40:00.640 --> 00:40:02.810
including, of
course, Stackdriver.

00:40:02.810 --> 00:40:07.050
And what we announced-- what
Urs announced on stage yesterday

00:40:07.050 --> 00:40:11.520
was managed Istio as part of our
coming Cloud Services Platform.

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:16.900
We are just about to go
into alpha on managed Istio.

00:40:16.900 --> 00:40:20.190
And that's Istio automatically
installed, upgraded,

00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:22.110
and supported in GKE.

00:40:22.110 --> 00:40:24.150
So, you can get started soon.

